WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.270
Film Courage: What makes a great story?

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.910
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: Oh, my…what makes
a great story?

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:05.910
Wow.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.640
That’s a good question.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:16.000
I have a friend who gets on me because I don’t
care a lot about plot.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.200
And I think there are two camps (maybe?) there
are people who care about character and there

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:27.020
are people who care about plot.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:36.579
I think there just has to be a reality to
it for me because…you know it’s funny,

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:41.940
I was talking to my students about this, there
has been thing in recent years and it’s

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:49.280
not new but the last 5-7 years where the value
of the story is related directly to whether

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.799
or not the audience can not directly anticipate
what the ending is going to be.

00:00:53.799 --> 00:00:59.719
In other words the twist has suddenly become
the gold standard of a great story.

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:02.789
Because if I didn’t see it coming that’s
when it’s a great story.

00:01:02.789 --> 00:01:05.750
“You got me!”

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:10.900
I feel like that’s kind of cheap.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.870
So I feel like it has to be character-based,
the characters have to be real and I’m almost

00:01:14.870 --> 00:01:21.310
forgive a plot that is not so clever if the
characters are strong and real.

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:23.300
To me that’s my standard at least.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:29.200
Film Courage: So if the characters feel too
much like they are acting, even though it’s

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.280
a great story, you didn’t see the ending
coming, to you that doesn’t…

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:39.230
Jack: I think characters that serve the plot
are less interesting than characters that

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.870
motivate the plot.

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:46.490
Like there is a certain philosophy…I’m
certain you guys have talked about this…whether

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:52.560
it’s easy for me or it’s something I happen
to subscribe to but if you can create a character

00:01:52.560 --> 00:02:01.030
first…if you can create a character in a
certain number of situations, not necessarily

00:02:01.030 --> 00:02:05.190
outline the entire movie (although it depends
on the movie) but if you have a full character

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.019
that’s real and you put something in front
of them, in a way they will begin to write

00:02:10.019 --> 00:02:11.209
the movie.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:15.230
You know how they are going to react to whatever
they put in front of them because they are

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:19.780
now these dimensional characters as opposed
to let’s have this happen, let’s have

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:26.109
this happen, let’s have this happen and
make sure that they do these things that are

00:02:26.109 --> 00:02:27.579
pre-ordained.

00:02:27.579 --> 00:02:32.430
And I think that’s what creates a feeling
of artifice or I’ve seen it all before or

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.819
boring.

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:42.700
So to me if you create a real character whatever
happens as the result of those characters

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:47.340
interacting is bound to be more interesting
than something that has just been constructed.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.370
Like I was attached to a big studio movie
not too long ago.

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:56.400
And the script was green lit and everybody
loved it and I was reading this thing and

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.499
I’m like “I don’t understand this…?”

00:02:59.499 --> 00:03:01.900
And it’s not a complicated movie.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:04.970
This was a movie with dinosaurs and fighting
and all that.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:09.359
And I’m reading this thing and I’m like
“Why is this guy doing this?”

00:03:09.359 --> 00:03:14.269
And I went to the writer and I said “This
doesn’t really make much sense.

00:03:14.269 --> 00:03:16.779
In the first act you talk about how this guy
was scared of heights.”

00:03:16.779 --> 00:03:19.230
There was this whole dialogue about how he
was scared of climbing the mountain and then

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.349
later he is on a tightrope fighting a Pterodactyl.

00:03:21.349 --> 00:03:23.069
What’s going on with that?

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:26.230
And he goes “I just thought that tightrope
thing was cool.”

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:31.629
And I’m like “Yeah, but it doesn’t make
any sense.”

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:35.849
You set up this guy…you can’t just put
stuff in because it’s cool.

00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:36.889
I mean people do it all the time.

00:03:36.889 --> 00:03:38.010
You set up this guy

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:43.779
But as a director of this thing…I was looking
at it like how am I supposed to direct this

00:03:43.779 --> 00:03:48.209
thing with any kind of confidence or authority
if I don’t believe this thing?

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:51.209
So I think there’s a lot of writing where
“It would be cool if this happens.

00:03:51.209 --> 00:03:52.749
And let’s have this happen and that would
be cool too.”

00:03:52.749 --> 00:03:57.769
You have this pile up of cool shit but it
doesn’t have any resonance because it’s

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:02.219
not based in any kind of reality or at least
any kind of consistency.

00:04:02.219 --> 00:04:05.999
Does that make any sense?

00:04:05.999 --> 00:04:12.269
Film Courage: It does so that character’s
“arc” didn’t cross over to the tightrope

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:13.409
scene?

00:04:13.409 --> 00:04:17.980
Did the character go to a fear-based weekend
[to overcome his fear of heights]?

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:23.690
Jack: Or have him acknowledge that when it
got to the tightrope scene, he was scared…he

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:30.130
was overcome by…but it’s almost this negligence…not
even negligence, it’s almost like apathy.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:38.350
It’s like “Who cares whether it makes
sense as long as it’s cool.

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:44.880
And I think the coolest movies that have the
coolest shit still are weighted or rooted

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:50.230
in some sort of reality that they, that the
writer has believed and maintained as sort

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:57.550
of an assembly thing that just happened to
be exciting but don’t really trace back

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:59.950
to something or make sense for the character.

00:04:59.950 --> 00:05:02.060
That’s really what it is, does it makes
sense for the character?

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:05.580
Would he do that.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:10.650
So the example I always give to my students
is if we’re walking along the street and

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:16.120
all of the sudden a mugger jumps out and grabs
your bag and runs and that’s all we have

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.360
in terms of the only idea we can come up with.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.290
The next scene (if we know who we are) we
know what’s going to happen.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:30.740
Like for me if a mugger pulls out a, I’ll
probably fall down on the ground and cry and

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:32.470
weep and hope for it to be over.

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:38.390
On the other hand if I had created, if you’re
a former cop or you were in the war or…

00:05:38.390 --> 00:05:39.640
Film Courage: Navy Seal.

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:40.640
Jack: Or whatever.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:46.350
Or somebody who has been mugged a couple times
and carries brass knuckles and mace…whatever

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:53.390
the deal…what happens as the result of that…mugger
pulls a  and goes off running.

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:57.940
It’s either a chase or it becomes us going
to a bar or crying together, but at least

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:01.330
you know…or it’s a fight.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:05.950
But you don’t have the pressure of “Ok,
now I have to come up with the next thing.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:07.390
Now I’ve got to come up with the next thing.”

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:13.370
Because if the character is built, then they
will dictate to you in a way what will happen

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:17.130
which is actually less pressure on the writer
because now you don’t have the burden of

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:19.810
having to make up every thing.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:24.160
The characters are in fact writing the movie
to a certain extent.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:28.830
Film Courage: They talk about having that
dialogue test where if someone is just reading

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:32.300
a character and then people need to try to
figure out who is speaking and if your character

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:34.370
is authentic enough, we should all know.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.280
Jack: Be able to identify, yes.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:41.760
In fact there are things where, there are
situations where my writing partner Jim and

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:46.340
I were first coming up with one of our films
LA CUCARACHA way back in the nineties.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.880
One of the first images that came to mind
was this idea of a man in a wheelchair in

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.880
almost this TRUE GRIT sort of scenario where
he was rolling into this town sort of with

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.370
blazing in a wheelchair in Mexico.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.880
That was the first thing.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:02.360
We didn’t even know how it got there, but
that was the image and it was amazing.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.700
And we were like “Holy shit!

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:04.700
That’s amazing.

00:07:04.700 --> 00:07:05.700
We’ve got to make this movie.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:06.700
We’ve got to write this.

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:07.700
We’ve got to figure out.”

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:12.880
And the irony was by the time we had created
this character and scenario (this writer that

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.100
goes down to Mexico who is really not a tough
guy at all and everything goes wrong) by the

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:21.860
time we finish this finale which is the seed
of the whole thing.

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:27.330
As much as we wanted this bad ass thing to
happen, it didn’t make any sense any more,

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:28.450
it was like too big, it was out of character.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:29.450
It didn’t make any sense any more.

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:30.450
It was too big, it was out of character.

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.220
It didn’t make sense that this would happen.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:37.110
And we kept trying to force ourselves into
writing this thing and it’s like No…it’s

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:45.020
not going to be that because that’s not
true to the thing that we actually wrote.

00:07:45.020 --> 00:07:47.520
I mean it depends on your philosophy.

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.500
A studio is not going to give a  about
that.

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:53.670
A studio is going to say “Put the 
wheelchair thing in because it’s awesome.”

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:58.170
But a writer with any kind of conscious is
going to go “No, that doesn’t work or

00:07:58.170 --> 00:08:02.420
they are going to really have to find a way
to justify.”

00:08:02.420 --> 00:08:12.660
I always heard that that was Hitchcock’s
thing.

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:17.550
I always heard that Hitchcock came up with
a series of really cool set pieces like I

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:21.950
want to chase across the faces of Mount Rushmore
but I don’t know how it happens.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:26.740
And then he would lay it on the writer to
some how get…”I want a Mount Rushmore

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:27.740
thing.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.560
I want a crop dusting thing.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:30.590
That will be awesome.

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:36.890
I want to stage and shoot those things.”And
now it’s your job Mr. and Misses writer

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.510
to figure out how to make it make sense.

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:44.190
Of course he had the luxury of…the writer
still has to figure it out thought.

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:48.590
And when you watch NORTH BY NORTHWEST and
you look at it, you’re like “Wow, that’s

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:50.740
a lot of effort to get this guy.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:51.740
Really?

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:54.190
They are going to bring him out to a corn
field and they are going to try to get him

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:55.340
with a crop duster.”

00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:57.800
Why doesn’t someone just pop out and 
the guy?

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:58.800
Isn’t that easier?

00:08:58.800 --> 00:08:59.800
Should their just be a ?

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.140
Why is there a crop duster?

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.670
Well it’s because Hitchcock came up with
this awesome idea and the writer is going

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:14.130
“Okay well if I maneuver this enough, maybe
no one will notice this is insane.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:19.290
So I don’t know, I guess it really does
depend on the philosophy of the writer or

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:19.800
the filmmaker.

